# Terraform Modules :
• A terraform module is a set of Terraform configuration files in a single directory.
• A Single Directory with one or more .tf files can be considered as a module.
• One root module can have any number of child modules in terraform.
eg. Inside one project or module we can have / create multiple child modules ec2, S3, vpc, ...

Note :
› We'll be running terraform commands from root module and root module will invoke child modules for execution

› We had created a directory ( 01-tf-script ) and within it we had various modules like input.tf , provider.tf , output.tf or main.tf .
› We call it ( 01-tf-script ) as one module .
› What if we have to create multiple resources ?
eg. S3, RDS, IAM, EC2, VPC, ...

› If we write everything in main we'll have various input variables
--> It will be difficult to maintain
--> Though it is a valid approach it's hectic as well.

› We can make use of Terraform Modules here .
--> In Terraform modules we have one root directory , we specify provider.tf , main.tf , output.tf ( to get output from child modules )
--> We create another directory named modules within which we specify what all resources ( as modules ) we want . We can create n no. of directories here , within which we can define input.tf , main.tf and our output.tf while the provider.tf is at root level.

* tree -
sudo yum install tree -y
    - It can be used to understand file structure in a diagramatic representation .
        eg. tree 01-tf-script

› It's not necessary to use input and output variables , not compulsary for small scripts .
› At root level we write main.tf file from where we invoke the other output files or child main.tf files .
› We have output.tf at root level to invoke child output.tf files .
› * All the terraform execution will be performed at the root level with better maintainance .
› The modules within root module are called as child modules .

› eg.         05 - tf - script                    (--> root module)
             |
             |---- provider.tf
             |---- main.tf
             |---- output.tf
             |
             |____ modules                      (--> child)
                   |
                   |____ ec2                    (--> child)
                   |     |---- main.tf
                   |     |---- input.tf
                   |     |---- output.tf
                   |
                   |____ S3                     (--> child)
                         |---- main.tf
                         |---- input.tf
                         |---- output.tf

